title: Windows, Linux: Members of Local Groups
agents: windows, linux
catalog: os/security
license: GPLv2
distribution: Andre
description:
 This check monitors the members of local groups on Windows hosts, by default
 the local Administrators group. It needs the agent plugin
 {local_group_members.ps1}, which can be deployed and configured with the
 agent bakery rule {Local group members (Windows, Linux)}.

 The plugin reads the members with {Get-LocalGroupMember} and falls back to
 ADSI if the cmdlet is missing or fails. Built-in groups are addressed by
 their SID, so the plugin works on Windows installations of any language.
 Domain controllers have no local groups and report nothing.

 On Linux hosts the agent plugin {local_group_members.py} reports the members
 of privileged groups such as {sudo} or {wheel}, the users and groups named in
 the sudoers files as group {sudoers}, and the accounts with UID 0 as group
 {UID 0}. sudo rules that come from LDAP or SSSD are not read.

 Without a rule the check is always {OK} and lists the members in the
 service details. With the rule {Local group members (Windows, Linux)} it can go
 {WARN} or {CRIT} if local users are members, if specific users or groups
 are members, if a required user or group is not a member, if a member is
 not on an allow list, or if members were added or removed since the service
 discovery. Members on the allow list are always
 {OK}.

 In the patterns of the rule the placeholder {$HOSTNAME$} stands for the
 Windows computer name of the host, on Linux for its short host name.

 If the group no longer exists or its members cannot be read, the check is
 {UNKNOWN}; this state is configurable.

 The check reports the number of users and of groups among the members as
 metrics.

 The members are also added to the HW/SW inventory below
 {Software > Operating system}.

item:
 The name of the group. Built-in groups have their English name on all
 hosts, e.g. {Administrators} or {Remote Desktop Users}. On Linux the group
 name, {sudoers} or {UID 0}.

discovery:
 One service is created for each configured group that exists on the host.
 On Linux {sudoers} gets a service if the host has a sudoers file.
